<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">M. Visvesvaraya</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1861–1962</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Mokshagundam Visvesvaraya</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">M. Visvesvaraya was an Indian civil engineer and statesman who served as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diwan">diwan</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mysore">Mysore</a> from 1912 to 1918. He designed flood-control and irrigation works across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, directed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna-raja-sagara">Krishna Raja Sagara</a> dam and promoted industries, banks and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-mysore">University of Mysore</a>. He received the Bharat Ratna in 1955, and his birthday, 15 September, is celebrated as Engineers' Day in India.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/m-visvesvaraya.jpg" alt="M. Visvesvaraya" width="320" height="400" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vishveshvarayya_in_his_30%27s.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
